---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getAccessoryPage } from '../../lib/db';

const featured = getAccessoryPage('humidors', 'case-elegance-renzo');
---

<Layout
  title="Best Humidors for Beginners | StickScore Guides"
  description="What to actually look for in your first humidor, and our current top pick."
>
  <div class="home-hero">
    <div class="cigar-hero__eyebrow">Buying Guide</div>
    <h1 class="home-hero__title">Best Humidors for Beginners</h1>
  </div>

  <section class="section summary-review">
    <p>
      A humidor's only job is holding your cigars at roughly 65-70% relative humidity, but the
      right first humidor also has to survive a beginner's learning curve — overfilling it,
      forgetting to check it, not knowing what "seasoning" means yet. Here's what actually matters
      when you're starting out, not just what looks nice on a shelf.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">What to look for</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>A humidification system you can't mess up.</strong> Traditional foam humidifiers
      need frequent refilling and are easy to over- or under-charge. Beginners generally do better
      with a system that's more forgiving — gel-based or two-way humidity packs (like Boveda) that
      hold a target RH automatically, or a manufacturer's own pre-mixed solution designed not to
      need constant adjustment.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A hygrometer you can actually trust.</strong> Cheap analog hygrometers are
      notoriously inaccurate out of the box. A pre-calibrated digital hygrometer, or one you
      calibrate yourself with a simple salt-test, removes a lot of first-humidor anxiety.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A seal that actually seals.</strong> A loose lid lets humidity leak out no matter how
      good your humidification system is. Magnetic closures and well-fitted hinges matter more
      than how ornate the finish looks.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Realistic capacity.</strong> It's tempting to buy small and "upgrade later," but a
      humidor that's mostly empty holds humidity less consistently than one that's reasonably full.
      Buying for slightly more than your current collection, not double it, tends to work better.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">Our current pick</h2>
    <p class="placeholder-note">
      This list will grow as more humidors clear our sourcing bar — right now, one has enough
      independent reviews to feature here.
    </p>
    {featured && (
      <a class="cigar-card" href={`/accessories/humidors/${featured.accessory.slug}/`}>
        <div class="cigar-card__brand">{featured.accessory.brand}</div>
        <div class="cigar-card__name">{featured.accessory.model}</div>
        <div class="cigar-card__score">
          AccScore {featured.accessory.acc_score != null ? Math.round(featured.accessory.acc_score) : 'insufficient data'}
        </div>
      </a>
    )}
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">A note on cost</h2>
    <p>
      A good beginner humidor doesn't need to be expensive — the fundamentals (a decent seal,
      a workable humidification system, an accurate hygrometer) show up at a wide range of price
      points. See our <a href="/accessories/humidors/">full humidors category</a> as more get added,
      and our guides on <a href="/guides/best-humidors-under-100/">humidors under $100</a> and
      <a href="/guides/best-humidors-under-200/">under $200</a> for budget-specific picks.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
